[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I tested inter-department connectivity end-to-end and could not reproduce any outage. From the Computer Science head’s PC (cs_hod), all IT department hosts are reachable: cs_hod -> it_hod (192.168.1.2) showed 0% loss with sub-4 ms RTTs, and cs_hod -> it_lab1..4 (192.168.1.3–1.6) all responded normally. In the reverse direction, from the IT head’s PC (it_hod), all CS lab hosts responded: it_hod -> cs_lab1..4 (192.168.2.3–2.6) each returned 0% loss with sub-4 ms RTTs. This demonstrates that L3 routing across router0 between lan_it (192.168.1.0/24) and lan_cs (192.168.2.0/24) is functioning, and both switch uplinks to router0 are passing traffic.

Mechanism: If a layer-3 or ACL issue existed on router0 or either switch uplink, pings across subnets would fail consistently in one or both directions. Instead, bi-directional ICMP is successful with normal latency, indicating healthy IP forwarding and no blocking policy.

Localization: No device shows current symptoms; the path between switch0–router0–switch1 is working.

Root cause: Not reproducible; the earlier report may have been transient (e.g., brief link flap, ARP cache issue, or a host-level problem that has since cleared).

Corrective fix: No changes required. If the issue recurs, capture timestamped evidence (failed pings from head PCs), verify each head PC’s default gateway, and check router0 and both switch uplinks for interface errors or flaps; consider enabling basic link monitoring/alerts.
=== END ===